13:08:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get the suspicion, and there's definitely *some* truth to it—shoe companies aren't losing sleep over making soles last 50,000 miles, and repeat purchases are obviously good for business. But I don't think that fully explains the wear difference, for a few reasons: **The physics checks out independently** Rolling vs. scraping friction is a well-established, testable phenomenon that has nothing to do with shoe companies—it's the same reason skateboard wheels wear faster than bike wheels, or why dragging your feet wears out shoe soles faster than walking normally. You can literally see this yourself: the toe area of a shoe (where scraping/push-off happens) wears out way faster than the middle of the sole (which mostly just rolls/plants). That pattern shows up regardless of brand or price point. **Tire companies have the same incentive, but different constraints** If planned obsolescence were the main driver, tire companies—who also profit from repeat sales—would have every reason to make tires wear out fast too. But they can't, because tire durability is heavily regulated, publicly rated (UTQG treadwear ratings), and cross-shopped by consumers specifically on longevity. Shoe durability isn't regulated or advertised nearly as rigorously, so companies have more room to cut corners—but that's a difference in *market pressure*, not proof that the physics is fake. **Some designs genuinely do sacrifice durability for other benefits** Lightweight running shoes and foam midsoles wear out faster partly because they're optimized for cushioning and energy return, not just longevity—similar to how race car tires ("track tires") wear out way faster than regular all-season tires because they prioritize grip over lifespan. That's a legitimate engineering tradeoff, not just a scheme. So I'd say it's probably **both**: the rolling-vs-scraping mechanics are real and account for a lot of the gap, *and* shoe companies have less incentive to fight against that natural wear by using thicker, harder rubber, since faster wear conveniently aligns with their bottom line. It's not an either/or—it's that the physics creates the opportunity, and market incentives don't push back against it the way they do for tires.
